{"op":"add","path":"/elements/step3-insight","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Step 2 established that churn scales with growth momentum, raising the question of whether that link is mediated by adding more customers or higher-ARPU ones; Step 3 settles it in favour of arpu, which leads by 72.3 percentage points (R² 0.0527 vs 0.6878). Standardised betas confirm volume as the dominant coefficient in the combined model, leaving ARPU as a secondary signal. (Steps 2, 3)","variant":"insight"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/conclusion","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Churn is a structural, volume-driven loss rate — not a pricing problem or a signal of product-market misfit. Reducing net revenue churn requires improving early-lifecycle retention in the Enterprise plan where attrition is both largest in absolute terms and disproportionate as a share of that segment's MRR. Recommended next steps: (1) survival analysis by acquisition cohort to detect whether newer cohorts churn faster, (2) qualitative investigation of Enterprise cancellation reasons, and (3) A/B testing onboarding interventions targeted at high-volume low-tenure accounts.","variant":"body"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/__synthesis","value":{"summary":"Churn is most concentrated in the Enterprise plan, which averages 9650 in churn MRR and bleeds 6.97% of that segment's own gross MRR (Step 1). Across all months, churn tracks growth almost perfectly — Pearson r of 0.999 — meaning it is a structural loss rate applied to an expanding base, not an accelerating crisis (Step 2). The root mechanism is customer volume, not ARPU: volume explains 0.0527 of churn variance versus 0.6878 for ARPU alone, confirming the lever is onboarding and early retention at scale (Step 3).","conclusion":"Churn is a structural, volume-driven loss rate — not a pricing problem or a signal of product-market misfit. Reducing net revenue churn requires improving early-lifecycle retention in the Enterprise plan where attrition is both largest in absolute terms and disproportionate as a share of that segment's MRR. Recommended next steps: (1) survival analysis by acquisition cohort to detect whether newer cohorts churn faster, (2) qualitative investigation of Enterprise cancellation reasons, and (3) A/B testing onboarding interventions targeted at high-volume low-tenure accounts."}}
